  "worked_solution": "Recall that if $A=QR$ is a  QR factorization of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n$$\n{A}_{m\\times n}={Q}_{m\\times n}\\times {R}_{n\\times n},\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $Q$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\n- $R$ is an $n\\times n$ upper triangular matrix\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. $Q$ should be an $m\\times n$ matrix, so $Q$ is not necessarily a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. $Q$ should be an orthogonal matrix, not necessarily an upper triangular matrix.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $R$ must be an upper triangular matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
